Relevant columns & their potential usage for the requested comparison

NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_BUILDING  
• "CLASS": high‑level building classification to be compared.  
• "SUBCLASS": more granular building classification to be compared.  
• "SURFACE_AREA_SQ_M": surface area that must be totalled.  
• "ID": unique id – useful when counting buildings.  
• "GEO_CORDINATES": GEOGRAPHY polygon – lets us decide whether a building lies inside Amsterdam or Rotterdam by spatial overlay with the administrative boundary.  
• "NAMES": can occasionally contain building names (not essential here, but good to inspect).  

NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ADMINISTRATIVE  
• "ADMIN_LEVEL": hierarchy level (city / municipality normally = 8).  
• "CLASS"/"SUBCLASS": kind of administrative area (e.g., “boundary”).  
• "NAMES": Variant field that stores locality names such as “Amsterdam” or “Rotterdam”.  
• "GEO_CORDINATES": GEOGRAPHY polygon of the administrative area – required for the spatial join with buildings.  

Typical conditions you might need  
• Filter administrative rows that represent municipalities: "ADMIN_LEVEL" = '8' (or 7, depending on OSM encoding).  
• Filter administrative rows whose "NAMES" (any language key) ILIKE '%Amsterdam%' or '%Rotterdam%'.  
• Spatial containment: ST_CONTAINS(a."GEO_CORDINATES", b."GEO_CORDINATES") OR  
  ST_CONTAINS(a."GEO_CORDINATES", ST_CENTROID(b."GEO_CORDINATES")).  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (simple → complex) that surface all the elements you will need before writing the final comparison query.  
